WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.700
It's finally here, you guys!

00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:04.880
"Game of Thrones" premiered back in the spring of 2011,

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:07.500
and now after eight years,

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:09.320
(twenty-three, if you're a fan of the books)

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:13.220
we are finally getting the answer 
we've all been waiting for:

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:17.590
Who will win CLEGANEBOWL?

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:19.480
It's a battle of the Titans!

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:23.660
In one corner, we've got a man who 
can crush your head like a Gusher,

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:26.880
and in the other we've got a man 
who really likes his chickens.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:30.960
It's the meme the writers have 
reluctantly included for fanservice,

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.320
but nevertheless, GET HYPE!

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:37.480
Oh, and I guess we'll also get to find out 
who gets to rule Westeros.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:38.300
So that's cool...

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:42.140
Hello Internet! Welcome to Film Theory!

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:45.280
The show where the night is dark, and full of theories!

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:48.620
You guys, I can't believe that
"Game of Thrones" is nearly over.

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:53.099
I know, I know that we're gonna get 
ourselves a variety of spin-offs and whatnot,

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:56.520
But we can never go back to this time 
when we were first discovering

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:59.460
a fantasy series that was as rich as Lord of the Rings,

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:04.360
with the added bonus anxiety of thinking 
that any character could die at any time.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:06.839
And at this point, with only six episodes left,

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:12.180
it's impressive that "Game of Thrones" still feels like it could go in any dozen of different directions,

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:16.760
Will John and Daenerys save the world and 
become Westeros' hot young power couple?

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:18.800
Will Bran turn out to be the Night King?

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:22.720
Or will Podrick heal the world 
with his magical lovemaking?

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:25.580
"These ladies enjoyed him so much 
they gave him the time for free?"

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:28.140
No one knows!

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:30.900
Well, no one except for Sansa's friends.

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:38.820
It is probably the single most hyped series finale in TV history since Breaking Bad ended back in 2013.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:43.380
So much so that there are betting odds 
for who will rule Westeros at the end,

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:45.440
as well as who will be first to die in this new season.

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:49.420
Let's just say, looking at the odds, 
things aren't looking too hot for the Greyjoys.

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:54.140
And so of course, true to form, today's theory is all about how this whole thing is gonna end.

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:56.680
Now, is my theory today gonna tell you to run to Vegas,

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:01.739
plunk down your cash on Tyrion, Varys and Dany for the win-place-show to get yourself rich?

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:06.160
No, today's theory is about how 
Game of Thrones should end,

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:07.649
not about how it will end.

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:11.180
And I think that those are two very different things.

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:15.560
At this point, the narrative of Game of Thrones is constrained by a number of factors.

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:17.620
It's a victim of its own success.

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:21.780
I mean, sure, Game of Thrones isn't 
afraid of being dark and ripping fans' hearts out,

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:24.900
but HBO might now be reluctant to do certain things,

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:28.269
now that they want people to stay invested 
in "Game of Thrones" spin-off shows.

00:02:28.269 --> 00:02:33.300
They might feel like they need a more TV-friendly 
ending that leaves people wanting more,

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.800
or leave certain characters alive.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:38.520
Plus, who knows how much the end of the series will line up

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:41.319
with the way that George R.R. Martin 
decides to end the books.

00:02:41.319 --> 00:02:45.100
I mean if you're Martin, don't you kinda 
want the show to end a bit differently,

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:47.860
so that there's still some reason 
to read those last two books,

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:50.860
"Winds of Winter", and "A Dream of Spring" 
when they eventually come out?

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:54.120
If they eventually come out... 
they're never coming out, are they?

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:57.320
So today? I'm talking about the 
ending that should happen.

00:02:57.320 --> 00:03:00.519
Once you get past all the dragons and war and incest,

00:03:00.519 --> 00:03:03.720
What is "Game of Thrones" really about?

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:07.520
And what kind of ending would 
serve all those themes the best?

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.160
It may not be the ending that Westeros needs,

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:13.780
but it is the ending that Westeros deserves.

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:15.980
As Kate says in her prophecy for Daenerys

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:18.160
"To go forward, you must go back."

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:21.980
So to think about how "Game of Thrones" 
should end, let's take a look at how it begins.

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:24.420
The pilot of a show like "Game of Thrones" is important,

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:27.280
because it's likely to establish not just the characters,

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:29.679
and setting, and plot points that 
will endure throughout the show,

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:32.940
but also because they tend to hint at the themes and messages

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:35.240
that the show will explore throughout the rest of it's run.

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:41.980
In our inaugural episode, we establish three main houses: Stark, Lannister and Targaryen.

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:45.700
These are the ones that the show 
tells us to invest in at the outset,

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:49.260
and while plenty of other houses have become important over the course of the series,

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:51.540
the show, going into its final season,

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:54.089
is still focused on these three.

00:03:54.089 --> 00:03:55.460
The Tyrells are dead:

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:57.420
*explosion*

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:58.300
The Boltons are dead:

00:03:58.300 --> 00:03:59.660
*dogs eating Ramsay Bolton*

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:01.060
The Martells are dead:

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:03.820
"Your daughter will die here in this cell and you will be here watching"

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:04.600
The Freys are dead:

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:06.760
"Winter came for House Frey."

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:08.920
the Greyjoy's and Tully's are still kicking around,

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:11.089
but, be honest with me, do you really care?

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:15.920
No, every time I hear about the Greyjoys, 
my eyes roll into the back of my head.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:17.209
"What is dead, may never die."

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:18.880
What is dead, may never die?

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:23.240
More like what is dead should stay dead, 
and just stick on their own damn island!

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:27.020
They're distractions, they were never 
real contenders for the throne.

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:32.660
But not only are the Starks, Lannisters and Targaryens the first three houses we become familiar with,

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:37.680
each one demonstrates a major theme 
that ends up pervading the rest of the series

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:39.500
1: The price of power,

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:42.320
2: The failure of man's institutions,

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:44.780
and 3: The dangers of pride.

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:49.100
And by looking at the way the series 
explores these themes, and these houses,

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:54.640
we get ourselves a clear pathway to what 
should be the ending for the entire series.

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:56.680
Theme number 1: The price of power.

00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:59.220
From the earliest days of watching the series,

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:02.960
we may not have been able to tell a Maester Pycelle from a Barristan Selmy,

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:06.680
but one thing we all knew was the Lannister motto:

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:07.840
"A Lannister always..."

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:08.600
"Don't say it!"

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:10.160
"Don't f****** say it."

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:13.120
It was beaten over our head.

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:15.300
With a seemingly limitless amount of money,

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:18.290
they bought their way into power and prestige in Westeros.

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:21.760
They understand the cost of things,

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:25.259
and they understood that sometimes that cost is more than just a bag of gold.

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:29.540
More often than not, the price of their own lofty ambitions was human life.

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:31.169
In the pilot alone,

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:33.640
they poison the hand of the king Jon Arryn,

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:37.400
to prevent him from revealing the fact that Robert's children were in fact Jamie's,

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:40.780
and then they attempt to murder 
Bran for discovering the same exact thing.

00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:43.000
Whether they're pushing children from windows,

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:44.720
hijacking their enemies weddings,

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:47.480
or blowing up hundreds of people to avoid going to trial,

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:51.560
there is nothing that the Lannisters won't do to remain in charge.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:55.800
And while the juicy violence is certainly
a part of the reason why we watch the show,

00:05:55.800 --> 00:06:02.780
a huge part of the narrative is all about the debts that need to be paid when you take these sorts of actions.

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:06.420
The karma that results from this kind of lust for power.

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:10.180
Tywin Lannister masterminds 
the red wedding which kills Catelyn Stark,

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:15.260
only to have Petyr Baelish reciprocate by killing his grandson during the Purple Wedding.

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:19.940
Cersei may solve one problem in blowing up the 
Sept of Baelor at the end of season six,

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:26.760
but the karmic retribution she receives is that her last living child Tommen jumps to his death out of grief.

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:34.160
She achieves power, but at the cost of the only thing that truly mattered to her in her life, her children, dying off.

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:37.200
The Lannisters do indeed pay their debts.

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:38.969
And while it's most obvious with the Lannisters,

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:43.820
the price of power is something 
that pervades every element of the series.

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:48.340
Stannis is so consumed by his desire to be king that he's willing to murder his brother Renly,

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:50.240
and burn his daughter Shireen.

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:52.340
He still loses and dies.

00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:57.660
Daenerys' older brother Viserys arranges to have his sister married off to a bloodthirsty warlord,

00:06:57.660 --> 00:06:59.420
just so he can retake the crown.

00:06:59.420 --> 00:07:02.940
And in the end Virserys gets his crown, 
just not the way he intended.

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:07.000
Even the more honourable quests 
for power come with a huge price tag.

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:11.000
Few would argue that Jon Snow 
didn't hold the moral high ground,

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:13.409
against Ramsay Bolton during 
the Battle of the Bastards,

00:07:13.409 --> 00:07:18.119
but the show makes sure that we see
 that even justified conquest is awful.

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:23.280
Jon Snow doesn't want thousands of people to die, offering Ramsay a one-on-one duel instead,

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:27.460
but when Ramsay refuses, everyone 
on that battlefield ends up dying anyway,

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:32.600
so much so that Jon almost suffocates 
to death under a giant pile of corpses.

00:07:32.770 --> 00:07:33.820
And then you've got Daenerys,

00:07:33.820 --> 00:07:38.889
who genuinely has progressive ideas about abolishing slavery and demolishing class divisions.

00:07:38.890 --> 00:07:42.699
But for someone who talks about leaving 
the world a better place than she found it,

00:07:42.700 --> 00:07:45.340
she sure does love burning people alive.

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:51.600
The moral here is that everyone, 
good, bad or otherwise, has blood on their hands.

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:54.120
And sure, while you may be rewarded temporarily,

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:57.880
eventually, you're gonna have to pay your karmic debt.

00:07:57.880 --> 00:08:00.580
Theme number two: The failure of institutions.

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:04.260
The thirst for power is a big 
element to Game of Thrones to be sure,

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:09.700
but in reality, it's just a symptom of institutions that humans have put into place for themselves.

00:08:09.700 --> 00:08:12.820
Part of the reason Daenerys wants 
to break the wheel in the first place,

00:08:12.820 --> 00:08:17.620
is because the rules about how people 
are governed are unjust and lead to abuse.

00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:19.800
The system of royal succession, for example,

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:21.580
not only leads to infighting,

00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:25.700
but also gives rise to rulers
who have no business ruling.

00:08:25.700 --> 00:08:30.220
Joffrey? He cared more about torturing people with crossbows than he did about ruling.

00:08:30.220 --> 00:08:32.860
Tommen? He was around 13 when he took the throne.

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:34.900
Heck, even King Baratheon!

00:08:34.900 --> 00:08:38.480
I mean sure he was older, 
and not a homicidal sociopath,

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:42.400
but he readily admits that he
 was a warrior rather than a ruler.

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:46.560
"Lord Eddard Stark, I would name 
you the hand of the king."

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:47.780
"I'm not trying to honor you."

00:08:47.780 --> 00:08:52.920
"I'm trying to get you to run my kingdom while I eat, drink and whore my way to an early grave."

00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:54.640
The fight between Stannis and Renly?

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:58.480
Sure, Stannis had a better claim to the throne as the next oldest brother to the king,

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:00.880
but Renly Was probably the better option.

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:03.340
Having more public support and leadership ability.

00:09:03.340 --> 00:09:07.120
The systems devised by men are constantly failing.

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:09.260
The characters know that they're flawed,

00:09:09.260 --> 00:09:12.840
but they don't do anything 
to fundamentally change the system.

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:15.640
Even Daenerys herself is a part of this problem,

00:09:15.640 --> 00:09:16.540
I mean, think about it.

00:09:16.540 --> 00:09:18.530
She says that she wants to change everything,

00:09:18.530 --> 00:09:22.160
but at the same time she insists that 
she's the rightful claim to the throne,

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:24.640
simply because she's the daughter of a former king.

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:26.890
A former king who happened to be a homicidal maniac.

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:28.930
She says she wants to break the wheel,

00:09:28.930 --> 00:09:31.760
but apparently she doesn't 
want to break the wheel entirely,

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:36.240
she wants to keep intact the parts of the wheel that, you know, support her claim to the throne.

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:39.700
I mean nobody is really advocating 
for democracy in Westeros,

00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:42.740
so how much are we really 
shattering that wheel, friends?

00:09:42.740 --> 00:09:44.880
And it's not just political systems either.

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:49.240
Look at another institution that plays 
a huge role throughout the story: marriage.

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:53.920
Daenerys, at least in the books, 
is a 13 year old who's married off to a warlord.

00:09:53.920 --> 00:09:57.780
Sansa's marriage to Tyrion is loveless 
and fails to protect her,

00:09:57.780 --> 00:09:59.860
her marriage to Ramsay is even worse.

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:05.799
Neither Robert Baratheon nor Cersei is happy in their marriage, even Catelyn and Eddard Stark.

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:09.900
What should be a loving marriage between 
two of the show's most noble characters

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:13.200
is tainted by her mistaken belief that he cheated on her,

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:16.960
leading to the birth of Jon Snow, 
and her lifelong resentment of him.

00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:22.320
The only truly positive love-filled marriage 
that we see on screen is with Robb Stark,

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:25.240
and um, that didn't turn out too well either.

00:10:25.240 --> 00:10:29.830
So if government and marriage aren't working too well in Westeros, then how about religion?

00:10:29.830 --> 00:10:32.320
No again, according to Georgie Martin!

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:36.819
Sure, the Lord of Light may be able to raise people from the dead, and give birth to shadow babies,

00:10:36.820 --> 00:10:40.360
but he also requires human sacrifices 
in exchange for winning battles,

00:10:40.360 --> 00:10:42.260
that, whoops, you end up losing anyway!

00:10:42.260 --> 00:10:45.360
Then, there's the entirety of seasons 5 and 6,

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:47.889
when Tommen yields too much 
power to the High Sparrow.

00:10:47.889 --> 00:10:50.660
It is one of the longest, and, in my opinion,

00:10:50.660 --> 00:10:53.340
most frustrating plot lines in the series,

00:10:53.340 --> 00:10:57.980
because it feels like filler that just
 slogs on for way too long.

00:10:57.980 --> 00:11:02.180
But when you actually stop and look at the themes of the entire story of "Game of Thrones",

00:11:02.180 --> 00:11:03.840
the purpose actually becomes clear.

00:11:03.850 --> 00:11:08.239
It's to show that no one, no institution is truly good.

00:11:08.239 --> 00:11:11.700
No matter how pious or noble 
the intentions might at first seem.

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:15.840
Which is exactly why the Starks 
are so important in the story.

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:20.740
Eddard Stark was a man defined by his belief of the inherent good of human institutions,

00:11:20.740 --> 00:11:22.280
and it gets him killed.

00:11:22.280 --> 00:11:27.240
His reliance on the arbitrary rules, and the way things are supposed to be done gets him beheaded.

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:31.280
Sansa desires a life of finery 
living with the nobles in King's Landing,

00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:35.880
but when she finally gets it, she winds up 
being abused by Joffrey, and then the Boltons.

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:41.360
It's only when she rejects society's rules for her, 
that she regains any sort of power in the story.

00:11:41.540 --> 00:11:47.480
Jon Snow is constantly trying to help the Night's Watch help themselves, and their response is to kill him for it.

00:11:47.509 --> 00:11:52.120
Now, in the aftermath of the Battle of Hardhome, where the tribe leaders pledged their weapons to him,

00:11:52.120 --> 00:11:54.378
he's basically the equivalent of the King-Beyond-the-Wall.

00:11:55.040 --> 00:11:59.800
He is a Wildling, and is more successful now than he ever was when he was wearing the black.

00:11:59.800 --> 00:12:02.220
But, perhaps the most obvious example is Arya.

00:12:02.220 --> 00:12:05.840
At the beginning of the story, Arya 
admires her sword master Syrio Forel,

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:08.988
a water dancer with perfect stances and elegant moves.

00:12:09.180 --> 00:12:11.640
The Hound quickly disabuses her of this.

00:12:11.720 --> 00:12:13.480
"Who taught you that shite?"

00:12:13.480 --> 00:12:14.860
"The greatest swordsman who ever lived."

00:12:14.860 --> 00:12:16.660
"Alright, you have a sword."

00:12:16.660 --> 00:12:18.400
"Let's see what he taught you."

00:12:18.400 --> 00:12:19.520
"Your friend's dead."

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:21.660
"Meryn Trant's not,

00:12:21.660 --> 00:12:25.340
"because Trant had armour and a big f****** sword."

00:12:25.340 --> 00:12:31.100
she then moves on to overturn the organization of the Faceless Men to become a merciless assassin,

00:12:31.100 --> 00:12:35.220
following her own rules and taking 
on any identity she chooses,

00:12:35.220 --> 00:12:39.980
and in the process, she literally
 has to reject her identity as a Stark.

00:12:39.980 --> 00:12:40.880
"Who are you?"

00:12:40.880 --> 00:12:41.540
"No one."

00:12:41.540 --> 00:12:45.040
It's only when the Starks deny 
their name and their upbringing,

00:12:45.040 --> 00:12:48.000
and their house's belief on the safety of institutions,

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:50.500
that they're finally able to survive in this world.

00:12:50.500 --> 00:12:56.760
Looking across the series, all the systems 
are unjust, unfair and destructive.

00:12:56.760 --> 00:13:00.680
Human society fails over and over again,

00:13:00.680 --> 00:13:04.420
which leads us to theme 
number 3: The dangers of pride.

00:13:04.420 --> 00:13:09.180
Potential threats to our beloved characters are telegraphed everywhere in this show.

00:13:09.180 --> 00:13:13.400
Catelyn actively warns Robb Stark to stay true to his marriage promise to the Freys,

00:13:13.400 --> 00:13:16.200
"Walder Frey is a dangerous man to cross."

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:17.520
"I know that."

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:19.480
"You gave him your word."

00:13:20.020 --> 00:13:22.960
"Treat your oaths recklessly,
 and your people will do the same."

00:13:22.960 --> 00:13:24.600
And he doesn't, and winds up dead.

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:29.780
When Eddard threatens to expose Cersei's adultery, 
she turns the threat right back at him.

00:13:29.780 --> 00:13:32.660
"When the King returns from his hunt,
 I'll tell him the truth."

00:13:32.660 --> 00:13:36.420
"Wherever you go, Robert's wrath will follow you.

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:37.720
"And what of my wrath, Lord Stark?"

00:13:37.720 --> 00:13:40.620
"When you play the Game of Thrones, 
you win, or you die."

00:13:40.629 --> 00:13:42.560
He's arrested, and executed.

00:13:42.560 --> 00:13:48.060
The slave masters of Meereen are given a pretty sweet deal by Tyrion to take seven years to phase out slavery,

00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:49.520
but they're unwilling to cooperate,

00:13:49.520 --> 00:13:50.529
and say it with me now,

00:13:50.529 --> 00:13:52.900
they get themselves killed.

00:13:52.900 --> 00:13:59.060
In all of these cases, and so many more throughout the series, pride is the reason for their downfall.

00:13:59.060 --> 00:14:03.780
Robb, and Ned, and the slave masters were so confident that they were untouchable,

00:14:03.780 --> 00:14:05.660
that they ignored the warnings.

00:14:05.660 --> 00:14:09.699
When Jon Snow returned to Castle Black from his semester abroad with the Wildlings,

00:14:09.699 --> 00:14:12.700
he gives the Night's Watch the advice to let the Wildlings through.

00:14:12.700 --> 00:14:15.200
They refuse, and most die as a result.

00:14:15.200 --> 00:14:17.800
Speaking of Wildlings, let's not forget Mance Rayder,

00:14:17.800 --> 00:14:20.540
the King-Beyond-The-Wall, their unofficial ruler.

00:14:20.540 --> 00:14:24.060
A man who refuses to bend his knee to anyone else.

00:14:24.060 --> 00:14:26.980
That pride costs him his life,

00:14:26.980 --> 00:14:28.660
and the lives of many of his followers.

00:14:28.660 --> 00:14:30.420
"Mance Rayder was a brave man."

00:14:31.260 --> 00:14:31.960
"A proud man."

00:14:31.960 --> 00:14:33.140
"Never bent the knee."

00:14:33.140 --> 00:14:36.340
"How many of his people died for his pride?"

00:14:36.340 --> 00:14:38.280
Pride gets people killed.

00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:40.260
It gets them to not cooperate.

00:14:40.260 --> 00:14:42.520
It gets them to not listen to each other.

00:14:42.520 --> 00:14:45.140
It gets them to ignore the warning signs,

00:14:45.140 --> 00:14:47.210
and by the time they realize the danger,

00:14:47.210 --> 00:14:49.690
it's too late to do anything about it.

00:14:49.690 --> 00:14:54.760
Nowhere is this better exemplified than the last of our three houses: the Targaryens.

00:14:54.760 --> 00:14:56.629
Viserys' this time on the show was short-lived,

00:14:56.630 --> 00:14:58.760
but it was filled with misplaced pride.

00:14:58.760 --> 00:15:01.960
"I am a Khaleesi of the Dothraki!"

00:15:01.960 --> 00:15:04.640
"The next time you raise a hand to me,"

00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:07.280
"Will be the last time you have hands."

00:15:07.280 --> 00:15:09.420
And the would-be Dragon King ignores the warnings,

00:15:09.420 --> 00:15:12.500
and earns himself his long overdue golden crown.

00:15:12.500 --> 00:15:15.020
Daenerys is equally prideful though,

00:15:15.020 --> 00:15:17.389
and pays for it dearly on multiple occasions.

00:15:17.390 --> 00:15:22.160
It's actually the entire point of another 
of my less than favorite plot lines: Meereen.

00:15:22.160 --> 00:15:25.400
In Meereen, she struggles against her pride constantly,

00:15:25.400 --> 00:15:27.540
as she tries to rule over slavers Bay.

00:15:27.540 --> 00:15:30.380
On one hand, her treatment 
of the Masters wins her no favors.

00:15:30.380 --> 00:15:34.200
"I've ordered Daario to execute every master in Yunkai."

00:15:34.200 --> 00:15:39.379
"Herding the masters into pens, and slaughtering them by the thousands is also treating men like beasts."

00:15:39.380 --> 00:15:41.940
"There's good and evil on both sides in every war."

00:15:41.940 --> 00:15:45.700
"Slavery is real. I will end those behind it."

00:15:45.710 --> 00:15:50.479
But at the same time, she loses the popular vote when she decides to publicly execute her advisor.

00:15:50.480 --> 00:15:52.420
*crowd booing*

00:15:52.420 --> 00:15:53.140
*axe noise*

00:15:54.920 --> 00:15:56.020
*crowd hissing*

00:15:56.020 --> 00:15:57.780
And of course, there's the fighting pits.

00:15:57.780 --> 00:16:02.260
Despite being advised by others to uphold 
this ancient tradition for the good of people,

00:16:02.260 --> 00:16:03.980
she thinks she knows better,

00:16:03.980 --> 00:16:06.200
refuses to heed the concerns of her team,

00:16:06.200 --> 00:16:10.140
and opens the door to more 
animosity between her, and the city.

00:16:10.140 --> 00:16:11.700
"They did ask for some concessions."

00:16:11.700 --> 00:16:12.560
"Concessions."

00:16:12.560 --> 00:16:14.540
"Politics is the art of compromise, your grace."

00:16:14.540 --> 00:16:16.190
"I'm not a politician, I'm a queen."

00:16:16.190 --> 00:16:19.140
"It's easier to rule happy subjects, than angry ones."

00:16:19.140 --> 00:16:23.940
In each case, a prideful decision from her comes with heavy negative consequences.

00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:26.380
And now, as we race towards the finish of the show,

00:16:26.380 --> 00:16:30.060
pride is once again a huge focus around her storylines,

00:16:30.060 --> 00:16:34.360
demanding that Jon Snow kneel to her as Queen before she'll lend him any favor.

00:16:34.360 --> 00:16:36.619
"I will fight for you, when you bend the knee."

00:16:36.700 --> 00:16:39.620
"My people won't accept a southern ruler."

00:16:39.620 --> 00:16:41.300
"They chose you to lead them."

00:16:41.300 --> 00:16:44.240
"Isn't their survival more important than your pride?"

00:16:44.240 --> 00:16:46.280
Here's why all this matters.

00:16:46.280 --> 00:16:53.100
Throughout the entirety of the series, everyone has ignored the real threat to Westeros - the White Walkers.

00:16:53.100 --> 00:16:57.000
Remember how I said that pilot episodes tend to kick off all your important characters and themes?

00:16:57.000 --> 00:17:00.500
Well in the opening minutes of the show, what are we shown?

00:17:00.500 --> 00:17:02.820
Not any of the houses, not King's Landing.

00:17:02.820 --> 00:17:05.979
Heck, not even characters that 
live through the opening moments!

00:17:05.980 --> 00:17:11.380
No, we are shown the danger of the White Walkers, as they butcher a scouting party.

00:17:11.380 --> 00:17:15.320
Even before that iconic theme music 
plays for the first time,

00:17:15.320 --> 00:17:18.380
it is the White Walkers that are getting top billing.

00:17:18.380 --> 00:17:23.300
Later on that same episode, we see Ned
 make his first crucial decision of the series,

00:17:23.300 --> 00:17:27.099
and by extension, his first crucial mistake of the series.

00:17:27.100 --> 00:17:31.089
He has to choose what to do with the deserter who escaped the White Walker attack with his life.

00:17:31.300 --> 00:17:35.440
Ned, being a man of rules and institutions, decides to behead him,

00:17:35.440 --> 00:17:38.400
despite the boy's warnings of the White Walker threat.

00:17:38.400 --> 00:17:39.520
"I know I broke my oath."

00:17:39.520 --> 00:17:40.440
"I saw what I saw."

00:17:40.440 --> 00:17:41.650
"I saw the White Walkers."

00:17:41.650 --> 00:17:42.800
"People need to know."

00:17:42.800 --> 00:17:44.420
"Is it true he saw the White Walkers?"

00:17:44.420 --> 00:17:46.840
"The White Walkers have been gone for thousands of years."

00:17:46.840 --> 00:17:51.300
And from that point forward, everyone
 just ignores the ice zombie threat.

00:17:51.300 --> 00:17:52.780
Shortly after Eddard's beheading,

00:17:52.780 --> 00:17:58.140
Alistair Thorn is sent to King's Landing with a zombie hand to show off, and no one cares.

00:17:58.140 --> 00:18:03.160
The Nights Watch meanwhile, is sending out dozens of messages to all the houses asking for help,

00:18:03.160 --> 00:18:06.130
and all of the messages get ignored for months.

00:18:06.230 --> 00:18:11.770
Samwell Tarly tells the Maesters in The Citadel that he has actually seen these things and that the threat is real,

00:18:11.840 --> 00:18:14.020
and they choose to just sit on their hands.

00:18:14.180 --> 00:18:17.800
Winter was coming, and coming, and still coming,

00:18:17.800 --> 00:18:19.509
and took a really long time to get there,

00:18:19.510 --> 00:18:21.120
but no one cared.

00:18:21.120 --> 00:18:22.620
No one prepared.

00:18:22.620 --> 00:18:27.380
They fought petty little battles against each other, sacrificing thousands of lives,

00:18:27.380 --> 00:18:30.640
just to decide who got to sit on 
a really uncomfortable looking chair,

00:18:30.640 --> 00:18:33.720
and now, the karmic debt is coming due.

00:18:33.720 --> 00:18:37.900
In the final hours, Cersei sees 
an undead Wight point blank,

00:18:37.900 --> 00:18:42.060
and her pride prevents her from 
partnering with rivals to the throne.

00:18:42.060 --> 00:18:44.600
"In return, the King in the North will extend this truce."

00:18:44.600 --> 00:18:46.240
"He will remain in the North where he belongs."

00:18:46.240 --> 00:18:47.470
"I cannot give you what you ask."

00:18:47.470 --> 00:18:49.560
"I cannot serve two Queens."

00:18:49.560 --> 00:18:50.980
"Then there is nothing left to discuss."

00:18:50.980 --> 00:18:52.260
"The dead will come North first."

00:18:52.260 --> 00:18:53.140
"Enjoy dealing with them."

00:18:53.140 --> 00:18:55.020
"We will deal with whatever is left of you."

00:18:55.020 --> 00:18:56.680
If this show has taught us anything,

00:18:56.680 --> 00:19:00.060
it's that everyone tends to die 
when people don't cooperate.

00:19:00.060 --> 00:19:03.960
So what kind of ending best fits with what Game of Thrones is trying to tell us?

00:19:03.960 --> 00:19:05.840
How should Game of Thrones end?

00:19:05.860 --> 00:19:09.140
Well, if we're looking at it from 
a purely thematic standpoint,

00:19:09.140 --> 00:19:11.240
the White Walkers should wipe out everyone.

00:19:11.240 --> 00:19:13.450
Mankind hasn't learned it's lesson.

00:19:13.450 --> 00:19:15.920
We either work together to all survive,

00:19:15.920 --> 00:19:17.480
or we all end up dying.

00:19:17.480 --> 00:19:21.360
People continue to be disloyal, brutal, willfully ignorant.

00:19:21.360 --> 00:19:23.530
They rely on systems that lead to abuse.

00:19:23.530 --> 00:19:27.300
They're prideful, and that pride 
prevents them from working together.

00:19:27.300 --> 00:19:29.589
So who deserves to rule Westeros?

00:19:29.590 --> 00:19:30.500
No one.

00:19:30.500 --> 00:19:32.940
I mean sure, Daenerys and John 
are working together now,

00:19:32.940 --> 00:19:34.780
but it's a little too little too late.

00:19:34.780 --> 00:19:36.760
They're all too self-interested,

00:19:36.760 --> 00:19:37.840
too short-sighted,

00:19:37.840 --> 00:19:39.440
too ethically compromised.

00:19:39.440 --> 00:19:45.320
The ending that makes the most thematic sense for Game of Thrones is for the White Walkers to win.

00:19:45.320 --> 00:19:48.249
For society to reset, in some way.

00:19:48.250 --> 00:19:50.460
It's not that mankind should lose,

00:19:50.460 --> 00:19:54.380
because Game of Thrones is some nihilistic 
universe in which choices don't matter,

00:19:54.380 --> 00:19:55.510
it's the complete opposite.

00:19:55.510 --> 00:20:00.120
It's that the choices mankind 
made in the lead-up to winter were wrong.

00:20:00.120 --> 00:20:01.510
They made the wrong choices.

00:20:01.510 --> 00:20:03.700
They weren't the responsible ones.

00:20:03.700 --> 00:20:07.119
And in the end, those choices should 
lead to their ultimate downfall.

00:20:07.360 --> 00:20:10.640
And if you don't believe that 
this is the ending that's deserved,

00:20:10.640 --> 00:20:15.640
remember, it's mankind's fault 
that the White Walkers exist in the first place.

00:20:15.641 --> 00:20:18.540
In the Season 4 finale episode 'The Children',

00:20:18.540 --> 00:20:24.160
Bran learns that the White Walkers were created by the Children of the Forest thousands of years ago,

00:20:24.160 --> 00:20:28.760
but the reason they created them was to defend against the destructive force

00:20:28.760 --> 00:20:31.780
that was invading Westeros - the first men.

00:20:31.780 --> 00:20:37.120
Men landed in Westeros, and immediately began slaughtering the indigenous races.

00:20:37.120 --> 00:20:41.080
In this way, the fall of Westeros 
is as much karma as it is anything else!

00:20:41.180 --> 00:20:45.140
There would be a karmic debt 
to pay for mankind's brutality.

00:20:45.140 --> 00:20:47.460
For their unjust systems to be wiped out.

00:20:47.460 --> 00:20:50.260
For their inability to cooperate leading to their demise,

00:20:50.260 --> 00:20:55.580
at the hands of a unified singular White Walker force, that wouldn't exist if it wasn't for them.

00:20:55.580 --> 00:20:58.340
One that the show's creators decided to show us,

00:20:58.340 --> 00:21:01.440
before we met any other characters in the series.

00:21:01.440 --> 00:21:06.460
What makes the most sense is 
for winter to come for all mankind.

00:21:06.460 --> 00:21:08.160
Will my prediction come true?

00:21:08.160 --> 00:21:12.180
Well, it's hard to spin off a series 
when everyone's dead, so probably not.

00:21:12.180 --> 00:21:17.280
But you gotta admit, that would be one really memorable way to end a series.

00:21:17.280 --> 00:21:22.360
But hey, that's just a theory - a Film Theory!

00:21:22.360 --> 00:21:24.820
Aaaand cut!

